<!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     Section 4 — Engineering-Focused FAQ (AIO 长尾问答)
     ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="relative py-24 overflow-hidden" style="background-color: var(--theme-surface);">
  <div class="container mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 relative z-10">
    <!-- Section Header -->
    <div class="text-center mb-16">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center px-4 py-2 mb-4 text-sm font-semibold rounded-full border" style="background-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-primary) 10%, transparent); border-color: var(--theme-primary); color: var(--theme-primary);">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4 mr-2" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9.879 7.519c1.171-1.025 3.071-1.025 4.242 0 1.172 1.025 1.172 2.687 0 3.712-.203.179-.43.326-.67.442-.745.361-1.45.999-1.45 1.827v.75M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0zm-9 5.25h.008v.008H12v-.008z" />
        </svg>
        Engineering FAQ
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6" style="color: var(--theme-text);">
        Engineer-to-Engineer <span class="gradient-text-accent">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge51')}</span>
      </h2>
      <p class="text-xl max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed" style="color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-text) 60%, transparent);">
        Direct answers to the most frequent DFM and engineering questions from procurement engineers evaluating titanium machining partners.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- FAQ Items -->
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-5">

      <!-- FAQ 1: Stress concentration in thin-walled Grade 5 -->
      <div class="rounded-xl p-6 transition-all duration-300" style="background-color: var(--theme-bg); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-primary) 8%, transparent);">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold mb-3" style="color: var(--theme-text);">
          How to reduce stress concentration in thin-walled Grade 5 titanium aerospace components?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-text) 55%, transparent);">
          Stress concentration in thin-walled Ti-6Al-4V geometries is addressed through three coordinated engineering strategies: <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge52')}</strong> — specifying minimum internal radii of R 0.5–1.0 mm to distribute load paths and eliminate notch effects; <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge53')}</strong> — employing trochoidal toolpaths with consistent radial engagement (&lt; 10% of tool diameter) to minimize cyclic thermal-mechanical loading; and <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge54')}</strong> — alternating material removal passes to allow stress redistribution between cuts. FEA-validated toolpath simulations verify that peak von Mises stress remains below 60% of the material yield strength throughout the machining process.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ 2: DFM review turnaround -->
      <div class="rounded-xl p-6 transition-all duration-300" style="background-color: var(--theme-bg); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-primary) 8%, transparent);">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold mb-3" style="color: var(--theme-text);">
          What is included in a standard DFM review for titanium CNC parts?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-text) 55%, transparent);">
          Our DFM review covers six mandatory checkpoints: (1) Titanium-specific machinability assessment per material grade (<em>{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge55')}</em>); (2) Feature-based tool access analysis for deep cavities, undercuts, and small-diameter holes; (3) Tolerance stack-up evaluation against ASME Y14.5 GD&amp;T standards; (4) Thin-wall deflection prediction using finite element modeling; (5) Surface finish requirement validation against achievable machining parameters; and (6) Raw material utilization optimization — including buy-to-fly ratio calculation and alternative form recommendations (plate, bar, or near-net forging). A detailed written report with annotated drawings is delivered within <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge31')}</strong> of CAD submission.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ 3: Cycle time reduction -->
      <div class="rounded-xl p-6 transition-all duration-300" style="background-color: var(--theme-bg); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-primary) 8%, transparent);">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold mb-3" style="color: var(--theme-text);">
          How can BOZE engineering reduce cycle times for existing titanium production programs?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-text) 55%, transparent);">
          Our value engineering team conducts a systematic cycle time analysis across four dimensions: <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge57')}</strong> — converting conventional roughing to adaptive clearing with high-feed mills, reducing roughing time by up to 40%; <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.text18_process_consolidation')}</strong> — combining milling, drilling, and tapping operations on multi-tasking mill-turn platforms, eliminating secondary setups; <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge59')}</strong> — selecting grade-specific carbide inserts with optimized chip-breaker geometries for titanium; and <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge60')}</strong> — reducing part loading/unloading time through quick-change pallet systems. Typical first-pass cycle time reduction: <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge61')}</strong> without capital equipment investment.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ 4: CAD/CAM software compatibility -->
      <div class="rounded-xl p-6 transition-all duration-300" style="background-color: var(--theme-bg); border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-primary) 8%, transparent);">
        <h3 class="text-base font-bold mb-3" style="color: var(--theme-text);">
          Which CAD/CAM software platforms does BOZE engineering support?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed" style="color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-text) 55%, transparent);">
          Our engineering team works with all major CAD/CAM platforms. We accept native files from <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge62')}</strong>, <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge63')}</strong>, <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge64')}</strong>, and neutral formats including <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge65')}</strong>, <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge66')}</strong>, and <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge67')}</strong>. For CAM programming, we use <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">Mastercam</strong> (5-axis simultaneous, mill-turn, and wire EDM modules) and <strong style="color: var(--theme-text);">{t('cap.engineeringpage.badge68')}</strong> for complex freeform surface machining. All toolpaths are validated through full-machine digital twin simulation with collision detection, spindle load monitoring, and surface finish prediction before production release.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <div class="text-center mt-12">
      <p class="text-sm mb-4" style="color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--theme-text) 45%, transparent);">
